//archive.org/details/hesiods-theogony-translated-by-michael-heumann",license:"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0",license_status:"provisionally_approved",redistribution_status:"noncommercial_only_pending_license_authority_review",text_shown:!1}],iN=JSON.parse(`[{"subject":"Alcmena","subject_surface":"Alcmena","subject_type":"Person","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Heracles","object_surface":"Heracles","object_type":"Hero","passage_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml:950","reference":"Theogony line 950","source_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml","passage_text":"And mighty Heracles, the valiant son of neat-ankled Alcmena, when he had finished his grievous toils, made Hebe the child of great Zeus and goldshod Hera his shy wife in snowy Olympus. Happy he! For he has finished his great work"},{"subject":"Zeus","subject_surface":"Zeus","subject_type":"Deity","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Hebe","object_surface":"Hebe","object_type":"Deity","passage_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml:950","reference":"Theogony line 950","source_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml","passage_text":"And mighty Heracles, the valiant son of neat-ankled Alcmena, when he had finished his grievous toils, made Hebe the child of great Zeus and goldshod Hera his shy wife in snowy Olympus. Happy he! For he has finished his great work"},{"subject":"Hera","subject_surface":"Hera","subject_type":"Deity","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Hebe","object_surface":"Hebe","object_type":"Deity","passage_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml:950","reference":"Theogony line 950","source_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml","passage_text":"And mighty Heracles, the valiant son of neat-ankled Alcmena, when he had finished his grievous toils, made Hebe the child of great Zeus and goldshod Hera his shy wife in snowy Olympus. Happy he! For he has finished his great work"},{"subject":"Heracles","subject_surface":"Heracles","subject_type":"Hero","predicate":"SPOUSE_OF","object":"Hebe","object_surface":"Hebe","object_type":"Deity","passage_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml:950","reference":"Theogony line 950","source_id":"hesiod_theogony_perseus_xml","passage_text":"And mighty Heracles, the valiant son of neat-ankled Alcmena, when he had finished his grievous toils, made Hebe the child of great Zeus and goldshod Hera his shy wife in snowy Olympus. Happy he! For he has finished his great work"},{"subject":"Oeneus","subject_surface":"Oeneus","subject_type":"Person","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Deianira","object_surface":"Deianira","object_type":"Person","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:2.7.5","reference":"Library 2.7.5","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"And having come to Calydon, Hercules wooed Deianira, daughter of Oeneus. He wrestled for her hand with Achelous, who assumed the likeness of a bull; but Hercules broke off one of his horns. So Hercules married Deianira, but Achelous recovered the horn by giving the horn of Amalthea in its stead. Now Amalthea was a daughter of Haemonius, and she had a bull's horn, which, according to Pherecydes, had the power of supplying meat or drink in abundance, whatever one might wish."},{"subject":"Heracles","subject_surface":"Hercules","subject_type":"Hero","predicate":"SPOUSE_OF","object":"Deianira","object_surface":"Deianira","object_type":"Person","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:2.7.5","reference":"Library 2.7.5","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"And having come to Calydon, Hercules wooed Deianira, daughter of Oeneus. He wrestled for her hand with Achelous, who assumed the likeness of a bull; but Hercules broke off one of his horns. So Hercules married Deianira, but Achelous recovered the horn by giving the horn of Amalthea in its stead. Now Amalthea was a daughter of Haemonius, and she had a bull's horn, which, according to Pherecydes, had the power of supplying meat or drink in abundance, whatever one might wish."},{"subject":"Haemonius","subject_surface":"Haemonius","subject_type":"Person","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Amalthea","object_surface":"Amalthea","object_type":"Person","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:2.7.5","reference":"Library 2.7.5","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"And having come to Calydon, Hercules wooed Deianira, daughter of Oeneus. He wrestled for her hand with Achelous, who assumed the likeness of a bull; but Hercules broke off one of his horns. So Hercules married Deianira, but Achelous recovered the horn by giving the horn of Amalthea in its stead. Now Amalthea was a daughter of Haemonius, and she had a bull's horn, which, according to Pherecydes, had the power of supplying meat or drink in abundance, whatever one might wish."},{"subject":"Hera","subject_surface":"Hera","subject_type":"Deity","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Hephaestus","object_surface":"Hephaestus","object_type":"Deity","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:1.3.5","reference":"Library 1.3.5","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"Hera gave birth to Hephaestus without intercourse with the other sex, but according to Homer he was one of her children by Zeus. Him Zeus cast out of heaven, because he came to the rescue of Hera in her bonds. For when Hercules had taken Troy and was at sea, Hera sent a storm after him; so Zeus hung her from Olympus. Hephaestus fell on Lemnos and was lamed of his legs, but Thetis saved him."},{"subject":"Zeus","subject_surface":"Zeus","subject_type":"Deity","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Hephaestus","object_surface":"Hephaestus","object_type":"Deity","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:1.3.5","reference":"Library 1.3.5","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"Hera gave birth to Hephaestus without intercourse with the other sex, but according to Homer he was one of her children by Zeus. Him Zeus cast out of heaven, because he came to the rescue of Hera in her bonds. For when Hercules had taken Troy and was at sea, Hera sent a storm after him; so Zeus hung her from Olympus. Hephaestus fell on Lemnos and was lamed of his legs, but Thetis saved him."},{"subject":"Rhea","subject_surface":"Rhea","subject_type":"Titan","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Zeus","object_surface":"Zeus","object_type":"Deity","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:1.1.6","reference":"Library 1.1.6","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"Enraged at this, Rhea repaired to Crete, when she was big with Zeus, and brought him forth in a cave of Dicte. She gave him to the Curetes and to the nymphs Adrastia and Ida, daughters of Melisseus, to nurse."},{"subject":"Melisseus","subject_surface":"Melisseus","subject_type":"Person","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Adrastia","object_surface":"Adrastia","object_type":"Person","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:1.1.6","reference":"Library 1.1.6","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"Enraged at this, Rhea repaired to Crete, when she was big with Zeus, and brought him forth in a cave of Dicte. She gave him to the Curetes and to the nymphs Adrastia and Ida, daughters of Melisseus, to nurse."},{"subject":"Melisseus","subject_surface":"Melisseus","subject_type":"Person","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Ida","object_surface":"Ida","object_type":"Person","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:1.1.6","reference":"Library 1.1.6","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"Enraged at this, Rhea repaired to Crete, when she was big with Zeus, and brought him forth in a cave of Dicte. She gave him to the Curetes and to the nymphs Adrastia and Ida, daughters of Melisseus, to nurse."},{"subject":"Salmoneus","subject_surface":"Salmoneus","subject_type":"Person","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Tyro","object_surface":"Tyro","object_type":"Person","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:1.9.8","reference":"Library 1.9.8","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"Now Tyro, daughter of Salmoneus and Alcidice, was brought up by Cretheus, brother of Salmoneus, and conceived a passion for the river Enipeus, and often would she hie to its running waters and utter her plaint to them. But Poseidon in the likeness of Enipeus lay with her, and she secretly gave birth to twin sons, whom she exposed. As the babes lay forlorn, a mare, belonging to some passing horsekeepers, kicked with its hoof one of the two infants and left a livid mark on its face. The horsekeeper took up both the children and reared them; and the one with the livid (pelion) mark he called Pelias, and the other Neleus. When they were grown up, they discovered their mother and killed their stepmother Sidero. For knowing that their mother was ill-used by her, they attacked her, but before they could catch her she had taken refuge in the precinct of Hera. However, Pelias cut her down on the very altars, and ever after he continued to treat Hera with contumely."},{"subject":"Alcidice","subject_surface":"Alcidice","subject_type":"Person","predicate":"PARENT_OF","object":"Tyro","object_surface":"Tyro","object_type":"Person","passage_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml:1.9.8","reference":"Library 1.9.8","source_id":"pseudo_apollodorus_library_frazer_1921_xml","passage_text":"Now Tyro, daughter of Salmoneus and Alcidice, was brought up by Cretheus, brother of Salmoneus, and conceived a passion for the river Enipeus, and often would she hie to its running waters and utter her plaint to them. But Poseidon in the likeness of Enipeus lay with her, and she secretly gave birth to twin sons, whom she exposed. As the babes lay forlorn, a mare, belonging to some passing horsekeepers, kicked with its hoof one of the two infants and left a livid mark on its face. The horsekeeper took up both th